  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\n\nSurface low pressure over the Northern Rockies will become\nvertically stacked as an upper low moves east from southern\nBritish Columbia. This will allow rainshower chances to ramp up\ntonight, peaking over the central and eastern zones Sunday. Most\nof these storms will not cause much impact but a few in the\nafternoon could be strong with hail being the main threat.\n\nAnother surge of rainshowers look to move east across the southern\nzones Monday. Rain totals from the two impulses look to be \nheaviest over the E zones with a decent chance of receiving at \nleast a quarter inch, very helpful for these drought stricken \nareas.\n\nTemperatures Sunday and Monday will drop to below normal levels.\nEnsembles then point to a drier and warmer pattern emerging mid to\nlate next week as an upper ridge noses into the region. Look for \nsunshine and temperatures getting into the 80s in the western \nzones Thursday and nearly areawide Friday. \n\nThe next upper trough moves in late in the week with the chance \nof thunderstorms and a slight cooldown.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nWe let the NBM do it's thing today for the entire period, no \ndeviations were made today. But later shifts may want to blend\nCAMS in to get more specific with rainfall timing and amounts. TFJ",
